<h2 id="%f0%9f%92%ad-angelas-pro-tips-recipe-notes"   class="wp-block-heading" >💭 Angela's Pro Tips &amp; Recipe Notes</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>To avoid a lumpy filling</strong>, make sure your cream cheese is completely at room temperature before mixing. If you're in a hurry, you can microwave the unwrapped brick for 15-20 seconds on low to medium-low power to soften it up.</li>



<li><strong>Even though flour tortillas are more pliable</strong> than corn tortillas, a quick 15-second warm-up in the microwave <em>(wrapped in a damp paper towel)</em> makes them much easier to roll without cracking or tearing.</li>



<li><strong>Every 8 ounces = 1 cup of enchilada sauce.</strong></li>



<li><strong>While you want a layer of sauce</strong> to prevent sticking, don't go overboard. Too much sauce on the bottom before baking can lead to soggy or mushy tortillas. A thin, even coating is all you need!</li>



<li><strong>For the best texture</strong>, shred the rotisserie chicken while it is still slightly warm from the store. It pulls apart much easier than cold chicken. Be sure to discard the skin and any large pieces of fat for a smooth, creamy filling.</li>



<li><strong>Yes, I like to use the packaged shredded cheese</strong> for convenience BUT freshly shredded cheese melts much better. Packaged cheese also has anti-caking agents to prevent it from clumping <em>(which also inhibits a great cheesy consistency)</em>.</li>



<li><strong>If you're cooking for kids</strong> or anyone that's sensitive to spice <em>(or you're unsure)</em>, use 'Mild' RoTel and a mild enchilada sauce. If you want more heat, use the 'Hot' RoTel, some extra green chiles, and add a dash of cayenne to your taco seasoning.</li>
</ul>



